Comparison between Debian-based and RPM-based systems in Linux:

---

Debian-based Systems

Package Format: .deb

Package Manager: dpkg (low-level), apt (high-level)

Popular Distros: Ubuntu, Debian, Linux Mint, Kali

Repositories: Use APT repositories

Command Examples:

Install: sudo apt install package-name

Remove: sudo apt remove package-name

Update: sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade


Dependency Handling: Strong dependency resolution via apt

---

RPM-based Systems

Package Format: .rpm

Package Manager: rpm (low-level), yum or dnf (high-level)

Popular Distros: Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL), CentOS, Fedora, openSUSE

Repositories: Use YUM/DNF repositories

Command Examples:

Install: sudo dnf install package-name

Remove: sudo dnf remove package-name

Update: sudo dnf update


Dependency Handling: Also good, but historically weaker than Debian-based systems (now much improved)

---

Key Differences Summary

Feature Debian-based RPM-based

Package Format .deb .rpm
Tools dpkg, apt rpm, yum, dnf
Popular Distros Ubuntu, Debian RHEL, CentOS, Fedora
Dependency Mgmt Excellent via apt Good via dnf/yum
